00:07:59,500 --> 00:08:04,500
that I had over the past week or so with a very dedicated concern ScoutMom,

107
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:10,500
who told me the story of her son's involvement in Scouts and asked me to give him some advice.

108
00:08:10,500 --> 00:08:15,500
That's going to take up the remainder of the podcast, so let's get started. shall we

109
00:08:15,500 --> 00:08:39,500
Music?

110
00:08:39,500 --> 00:08:45,500
Write me a letter, send it by name

111
00:08:45,500 --> 00:08:48,500
Email. that is, folks.

112
00:08:48,500 --> 00:08:51,500
And here's an answer to one of your emails,

113
00:08:51,500 --> 00:08:59,500
Music.

114
00:08:59,500 --> 00:09:07,500
Well, as I shared before, this message comes from a very dedicated, concerned mother of a scout

115
00:09:07,500 --> 00:09:09,500
And it reads this way.

116
00:09:09,500 --> 00:09:15,500
It says: a while back, a friend shared a scoutmastercgcom post regarding the patrol method

117
00:09:15,500 --> 00:09:17,500
And it was a real ray of light.

118
00:09:17,500 --> 00:09:20,500
I had a frustrated introduction to Boy Scouts.

119
00:09:20,500 --> 00:09:23,500
This was not like my wonderful memories of Girl Scouts

120
00:09:23,500 --> 00:09:29,500
And I wasn't so sure I wanted my son involved if this was the way the program was going to be.

121
00:09:29,500 --> 00:09:35,500
Most of what I saw in our troop at the time was kind of a military dictatorship.

122
00:09:36,500 --> 00:09:41,500
I didn't see how this system possibly had a hand in creating some of the good men I knew,

123
00:09:41,500 --> 00:09:50,500
And the patrols in that troop seemed to be just another bit of this ridiculously regimented and almost angry setup.

124
00:09:50,500 --> 00:09:52,500
I read the article over and over

125
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:56,500
And the type of growth you were describing is what I wanted for my son.

126
00:09:56,500 --> 00:09:59,500
My entire attitude towards the scouting program changed

127
00:09:59,500 --> 00:10:04,500
And, by the way, in the podcast notes I'll link to that series of articles.

128
00:10:04,500 --> 00:10:10,500
My son grew so frustrated with the experience he was having he decided to change troops.

129
00:10:10,500 --> 00:10:15,500
Tuesday night he was elected as the senior patrol leader in his new troop.

130
00:10:15,500 --> 00:10:21,500
The scoutmaster of his new troop has a lot of good intentions but he has a very commanding presence

131
00:10:21,500 --> 00:10:30,500
And because of the frustrations, over time he's taken to leading things himself because it's easier and it's less messy.

132
00:10:30,500 --> 00:10:36,500
I know that the other adults involved would like to model the troop more after the patrol method,

133
00:10:36,500 --> 00:10:39,500
but this change is really going to be driven by the scouts.

134
00:10:39,500 --> 00:10:47,500
My son is working his way through your back podcasts, absorbing all of the scoutmastership in the true sense of the word that he can.

135
00:10:47,500 --> 00:10:56,500
I've asked him questions based on what he's heard so far in. his basic plan is to be as active as he can and try and encourage things to change.

136
00:10:56,500 --> 00:10:58,500
Which brings me to my question.

137
00:10:58,500 --> 00:11:05,500
You have a lot of content that will take a lot of time to go through and most of it is from the adult side.

138
00:11:05,500 --> 00:11:18,500
Do you have a particular podcast or article that you could point my son to? that would be helpful for him and his fellow scouts as they work to bring about this change in their troop.

139
00:11:18,500 --> 00:11:21,500
If not, do you have any general advice for them?

140
00:11:21,500 --> 00:11:25,500
I want to say thank you for your podcast and your blog.

141
00:11:25,500 --> 00:11:29,500
They've not only changed my view of Boy Scouts but enhanced my parenting efforts.

142
00:11:29,500 --> 00:11:34,500
I hear you in my head talking to other parents and quote you with credit often.

143
00:11:34,500 --> 00:11:37,500
Well, thank you so much for the kind words.

144
00:11:37,500 --> 00:11:39,500
I'm glad that I'm able to help.

145
00:11:39,500 --> 00:11:44,500
You pointed out something that I really don't have, because I don't hear from a lot of young people.

146
00:11:44,500 --> 00:11:57,500
I mostly talk with and communicate with other adult volunteers and scouting, so I don't have a lot of information that's aimed at youth leaders specifically.

147
00:11:57,500 --> 00:12:07,500
So we're going to take some time in this podcast and see if I can come up with a couple of ideas and some advice for youth leaders who might find themselves in the same position,

148
00:12:07,500 --> 00:12:10,500
And hopefully it'll be good advice for everybody. but we'll give it a shot.

149
00:12:10,500 --> 00:12:21,500
Presented with the situation as you've related it, I'm tempted to say the youth members of a troop trying to make changes to this nature are coming out of from the wrong end of things.

150
00:12:21,500 --> 00:12:29,500
Now I believe there's a way to make things happen and to strengthen the patrol method that can come from the youth.

151
00:12:29,500 --> 00:12:37,500
You have to be willing to do some work first, and we've got to have adult volunteers who are going to be receptive to these ideas.

152
00:12:37,500 --> 00:12:50,500
Let's talk about understanding the adults involved and your relationship to them as a youth leader, and how you offer constructive criticism that's aimed at resolving problems and kind of moving the ball forward.

153
00:12:50,500 --> 00:12:56,500
Now, before I go on, this advice assumes that you have a pretty good grasp of the patrol method.

154
00:12:56,500 --> 00:13:02,500
I'm not going to go into an explanation of it here, because it is something we've discussed many, many times.

155
00:13:02,500 --> 00:13:07,500
The series of articles about the patrol method I mentioned earlier would be a good place to start.

156
00:13:07,500 --> 00:13:15,500
I can also recommend a book called Working the Patrol Method and The Old Patrol Leaders Handbook by Green Bar Bill.

157
00:13:15,500 --> 00:13:19,500
I'll have some links on ways you can find both of those books in the podcast notes.

158
00:13:19,500 --> 00:13:31,500
So once you have a pretty good idea of what the patrol method ought to look like and you want to share this with adults and you want to kind of change the way that your troop runs, how are we going to go about doing this.

159
00:13:31,500 --> 00:13:36,500
You're trusting the adults involved with your scout troop to provide you with leadership,

160
00:13:36,500 --> 00:13:45,500
And adults volunteer their time to scouting for any number of reasons, but I don't think many of them would say that they were there to be guided by the scouts.

161
00:13:45,500 --> 00:13:48,500
So let's think about that for a moment.

162
00:13:48,500 --> 00:13:55,500
If you take the initiative and start talking to adults about these things, you're going to need to be prepared for them to react with surprise.

163
00:13:55,500 --> 00:14:01,500
It's almost like jumping out and yelling Boo,

164
00:14:01,500 --> 00:14:10,500
This is going to be surprising to them that a youth will take the initiative to really start discussing these things and to ask for changes to be made.

165
00:14:10,500 --> 00:14:13,500
This is not generally the way things work.

166
00:14:13,500 --> 00:14:21,500
It's not generally the way things work at your school, or if it probably doesn't work that way in most families,

167
00:14:21,500 --> 00:14:32,500
Adults are expected to lead and children are expected to follow, and scouting tends to turn that on its head a little bit.

168
00:14:32,500 --> 00:14:43,500
And while I know youth members in scouting are usually very quick to pick this up and to make it happen when they're allowed to, adults find it really, really difficult.

169
00:14:43,500 --> 00:15:00,500
So I'm going to ask you, as a youth, as a scout who has got a position of responsibility and is a leader. you're going to have to be pretty patient with these adults, because they may never have tried anything like this before.

170
00:15:00,500 --> 00:15:05,500
They're not going to have a whole lot of confidence in that this is going to work.

171
00:15:05,500 --> 00:15:11,500
I would just say from all the people that I talk to in general, that's the way that it is.

172
00:15:11,500 --> 00:15:16,500
So I want you to have some very measured expectations about exactly what kind of reaction you're going to get.

173
00:15:16,500 --> 00:15:24,500
I mean, they could get upset, they could be a little scared, they may be distressed because they feel they are supposed to be leading,

174
00:15:24,500 --> 00:15:29,500
And when scouts take the initiative, it can upset them sometimes,

175
00:15:29,500 --> 00:15:31,500
But that's not everybody.

176
00:15:31,500 --> 00:15:41,500
Now, if the adults who are volunteering for your troop are good leaders, they will accept and respect feedback from the people who are their leading.

177
00:15:41,500 --> 00:15:45,500
They'll listen carefully and they'll make changes if they're wrong.

178
00:15:45,500 --> 00:15:49,500
And if they are willing to listen well, that's a pretty good indication that you're in the right place.

179
00:15:49,500 --> 00:15:59,500
If they're not willing to listen, you know, if you can't even get started, you might have to consider that you might not be working with the right people,

180
00:15:59,500 --> 00:16:05,500
Because if you do not have genuine respect for those who are in authority over you and who are leading you,

181
00:16:05,500 --> 00:16:13,500
because I know that it's a little confusing to say that, because adults aren't supposed to be quote, leading unquote,

182
00:16:13,500 --> 00:16:15,500
But you understand what I'm saying.

183
00:16:15,500 --> 00:16:19,500
They're there to help, guide and instruct and mentor you.

184
00:16:19,500 --> 00:16:21,500
So that's a form of leadership.

185
00:16:21,500 --> 00:16:25,500
If you don't have genuine respect for the adult volunteers, you might be in the wrong place.

186
00:16:25,500 --> 00:16:29,500
If you can't find anybody, you may need to consider a move.

187
00:16:29,500 --> 00:16:31,500
Now, that's general advice.

188
00:16:31,500 --> 00:16:33,500
I know the story that I heard about.

189
00:16:33,500 --> 00:16:35,500
They've already moved troops.

190
00:16:35,500 --> 00:16:36,500
I understand that,

191
00:16:36,500 --> 00:16:38,500
But that's just general advice.

192
00:16:38,500 --> 00:16:44,500
This is one situation in your young life where you get to make the choice about this.

193
00:16:44,500 --> 00:16:53,500
You can't necessarily choose which school you're going to go to or which teacher you're going to have or who's going to coach the school team that you play on

194
00:16:53,500 --> 00:16:55,500
or direct the school play,

195
00:16:55,500 --> 00:16:58,500
But with scouts, you get to make some choices about that.

196
00:16:58,500 --> 00:17:02,500
If what is going on in one troop doesn't work for you, you can move,

197
00:17:02,500 --> 00:17:08,500
You can go to another troop and you can basically shop around until you find adults who are willing to work with you.

198
00:17:08,500 --> 00:17:13,500
So let's talk about the matter of respect for a moment, because respect is a pretty weighted word.

199
00:17:13,500 --> 00:17:15,500
I'm talking about genuine respect

200
00:17:15,500 --> 00:17:25,500
When I say that. I'm talking about respect that is not meaningless or perfunctory, because you know you're a young person and so everybody older than you,

201
00:17:25,500 --> 00:17:28,500
especially adults- you're supposed to have this profound respect for them.

202
00:17:28,500 --> 00:17:33,500
And some of that is just about courtesy and being polite and things like that.

203
00:17:33,500 --> 00:17:35,500
I'm talking about a genuine respect.

204
00:17:35,500 --> 00:17:38,500
You should be able to look at the scouts that you're working with and say:

205
00:17:38,500 --> 00:17:43,500
these are people who have my best interest at heart and I really respect that and I'm willing to work along with them.

206
00:17:43,500 --> 00:17:47,500
If you're looking at them and you're going, you know this is all I've got to work with.

207
00:17:47,500 --> 00:17:49,500
Like I said, consider a change

208
00:17:49,500 --> 00:17:52,500
Now. genuine respect is definitely a two-way street.

209
00:17:52,500 --> 00:17:58,500
You cannot expect to be respected if you're not willing to show respect. right,

210
00:17:58,500 --> 00:18:00,500
I think we all understand that.

211
00:18:00,500 --> 00:18:04,500
So to show some respect for the adults who are volunteering to help you,

212
00:18:04,500 --> 00:18:09,500
you have to kind of understand where they're coming from- and they can be coming from all over the map,

213
00:18:09,500 --> 00:18:17,500
But for the most part, other adults that I've worked with in scouting are really motivated by a sincere interest in helping youth.

214
00:18:17,500 --> 00:18:22,500
They may not understand exactly how to do that all the time, but that's what they're motivated by.

215
00:18:22,500 --> 00:18:28,500
So you have to assume goodwill on the part of the adults who are working with you,

216
00:18:28,500 --> 00:18:32,500
And if you truly and genuinely respect them,

217
00:18:33,500 --> 00:18:40,500
then you will find that they will reciprocate by truly and genuinely respecting what you have to say.

218
00:18:40,500 --> 00:18:42,500
Now let's talk about what you have to say.

219
00:18:42,500 --> 00:18:46,500
So, like I said before, if you have a good, solid understanding of the patrol method

220
00:18:46,500 --> 00:18:50,500
and you don't think some things are working in that direction,

221
00:18:50,500 --> 00:18:54,500
then you need to talk to the adults who are volunteering for your troop

222
00:18:54,500 --> 00:18:58,500
and you need to be very plain about the changes that you would like to see.

223
00:18:58,500 --> 00:19:01,500
And when I say very plain, I mean very plain,

224
00:19:01,500 --> 00:19:06,500
Without being aggressive, mean you know, or complaining.

225
00:19:06,500 --> 00:19:11,500
you need to state exactly what you think needs to happen

226
00:19:11,500 --> 00:19:14,500
and be willing to listen and have a discussion about those things.

227
00:19:14,500 --> 00:19:19,500
Now listen, you have heard people say things like: with all due respect

228
00:19:19,500 --> 00:19:25,500
before they say something, or no offense, but or don't take this the wrong way.

229
00:19:25,500 --> 00:19:30,500
But let me give you a bit of advice: Don't ever, ever say those things.

230
00:19:30,500 --> 00:19:35,500
Perfectly nice, well-intentioned people say those sorts of things all the time

231
00:19:35,500 --> 00:19:40,500
and they're a huge mistake because we all know exactly what they mean

232
00:19:40,500 --> 00:19:44,500
When somebody wants to talk to you and they begin a sentence with,

233
00:19:44,500 --> 00:19:49,500
with all due respect, that means, basically, I don't really have a whole lot of respect for you,

234
00:19:49,500 --> 00:19:53,500
So I have to mention it right here at the beginning of what I'm going to say.

235
00:19:53,500 --> 00:20:00,500
If they say no offense, it means that they realize what they're about to say is offensive,

236
00:20:00,500 --> 00:20:04,500
Right. And if they say don't take this the wrong way,

237
00:20:04,500 --> 00:20:08,500
that means what I'm about to say is so profoundly disrespectful

238
00:20:08,500 --> 00:20:10,500
you will not be able to take it the right way.

239
00:20:10,500 --> 00:20:13,500
If you want to show genuine respect for someone in authority,

240
00:20:13,500 --> 00:20:16,500
you will state what the problem is and you'll make it clear

241
00:20:16,500 --> 00:20:19,500
that you want to help make constructive change,

242
00:20:19,500 --> 00:20:22,500
And this is called constructive criticism.

243
00:20:22,500 --> 00:20:27,500
Offering a solution is the difference between constructive criticism,

244
00:20:27,500 --> 00:20:30,500
complaining or making accusations.

245
00:20:30,500 --> 00:20:33,500
Now, this kind of approach requires courage,

246
00:20:33,500 --> 00:20:37,500
but is infinitely more respectful than beating around the bush

247
00:20:37,500 --> 00:20:40,500
or slathering people with disingenuous praise

248
00:20:40,500 --> 00:20:44,500
or- the worst possible approach- trying to work around someone.

249
00:20:44,500 --> 00:20:47,500
So what am I talking about precisely? Well, how about this?

250
00:20:47,500 --> 00:20:50,500
You will go up to your scout master and you say:

251
00:20:50,500 --> 00:20:53,500
I don't feel I have the kind of authority I need to get my job done

252
00:20:53,500 --> 00:20:56,500
as a senior patrol leader or patrol leader or whatever.

253
00:20:56,500 --> 00:20:59,500
That's an example of a direct, unambiguous and therefore,

254
00:20:59,500 --> 00:21:02,500
to my lights- respectful statement

255
00:21:02,500 --> 00:21:05,500
And it would take a fair amount of courage to be able to do that.

256
00:21:05,500 --> 00:21:07,500
but a scout is brave.

257
00:21:07,500 --> 00:21:10,500
It's easy to say okay,

258
00:21:10,500 --> 00:21:14,500
But truly, you can follow that statement up with.

259
00:21:14,500 --> 00:21:18,500
I would like to discuss and agree on how and who makes decisions

260
00:21:18,500 --> 00:21:21,500
so we both understand what's going on, or something like that.

261
00:21:21,500 --> 00:21:25,500
And that's a good follow-up statement because it offers a solution.

262
00:21:25,500 --> 00:21:27,500
Do you see what I'm saying?

263
00:21:27,500 --> 00:21:31,500
A complaint is saying you don't let me do X,

264
00:21:31,500 --> 00:21:35,500
or I should be able to do this, or I should be able to do that.

265
00:21:35,500 --> 00:21:37,500
That's kind of a complaint.

266
00:21:37,500 --> 00:21:39,500
You are also referring to the other person in it.

267
00:21:39,500 --> 00:21:41,500
You see the difference between that

268
00:21:41,500 --> 00:21:45,500
and I don't think that I have the authority that I need to get my job done.

269
00:21:45,500 --> 00:21:48,500
It doesn't really mention the other person does it?

270
00:21:48,500 --> 00:21:50,500
And then you want to offer a solution right there.

271
00:21:50,500 --> 00:21:52,500
I'd like to discuss how we can figure this out.

272
00:21:52,500 --> 00:21:55,500
I have some ideas about how we could make this better

273
00:21:55,500 --> 00:21:57,500
if you're willing to talk about it.

274
00:21:57,500 --> 00:21:59,500
The other thing to consider is that there's a difference

275
00:21:59,500 --> 00:22:04,500
between criticizing a person and assessing a situation

276
00:22:04,500 --> 00:22:06,500
and offering constructive criticism.

277
00:22:06,500 --> 00:22:07,500
With a little practice,

278
00:22:07,500 --> 00:22:09,500
you learn how to separate problems from people.

279
00:22:09,500 --> 00:22:11,500
Now, this is not easy to do

280
00:22:11,500 --> 00:22:14,500
and most adults are not very good at it,

281
00:22:14,500 --> 00:22:17,500
because people do cause problems.

282
00:22:17,500 --> 00:22:19,500
But if we're going to solve problems,

283
00:22:19,500 --> 00:22:23,500
if we're going to meet challenges and reach goals,

284
00:22:23,500 --> 00:22:27,500
we have to understand that you, as a youth leader,

285
00:22:27,500 --> 00:22:29,500
and the adults who are volunteering

286
00:22:29,500 --> 00:22:33,500
are not two separate teams competing for a win.

287
00:22:33,500 --> 00:22:37,500
You are a single team competing against a challenge

288
00:22:37,500 --> 00:22:41,500
or a problem or working towards a goal.

289
00:22:41,500 --> 00:22:43,500
I mean, imagine there's a big pile of stones

290
00:22:43,500 --> 00:22:45,500
that need to be moved from point A to point B.

291
00:22:45,500 --> 00:22:47,500
You have a group of people to move them.

292
00:22:47,500 --> 00:22:50,500
Even if you think the stones are at point A

293
00:22:50,500 --> 00:22:52,500
because somebody made a mistake

294
00:22:52,500 --> 00:22:54,500
or acted out a malice towards you,

295
00:22:54,500 --> 00:22:57,500
there's little to be gained by talking about that

296
00:22:57,500 --> 00:23:00,500
or assigning guilt or trying to make somebody feel bad

297
00:23:00,500 --> 00:23:03,500
about the fact that, hey, all these stones are over here.

298
00:23:03,500 --> 00:23:07,500
How about you put everybody's energy into the problem

299
00:23:07,500 --> 00:23:10,500
by saying: how are we going to move these stones?

300
00:23:10,500 --> 00:23:12,500
It doesn't really matter how they got here,

301
00:23:12,500 --> 00:23:14,500
We have to get them over to point B.

302
00:23:14,500 --> 00:23:16,500
How are we going to make that happen?

303
00:23:16,500 --> 00:23:18,500
Because the only way you're going to move things forward

304
00:23:18,500 --> 00:23:20,500
is if you're working together,

305
00:23:20,500 --> 00:23:22,500
And to do that somebody has to begin

306
00:23:22,500 --> 00:23:24,500
by kind of picking up stones and carrying them

307
00:23:24,500 --> 00:23:26,500
and encouraging everyone to do the same.

308
00:23:26,500 --> 00:23:30,500
It takes courage, which is another way of saying being brave.

309
00:23:30,500 --> 00:23:33,500
It takes sensitivity to the way other people feel,

310
00:23:33,500 --> 00:23:36,500
which is being friendly and courteous and kind,

311
00:23:36,500 --> 00:23:40,500
And it takes someone willing to build a sense of team,

312
00:23:40,500 --> 00:23:44,500
because it's never us against them,

313
00:23:44,500 --> 00:23:47,500
It's never the youth leaders against the adults

314
00:23:47,500 --> 00:23:49,500
or the adults against the youth leaders.

315
00:23:49,500 --> 00:23:54,500
It's a case of all of us against it,

316
00:23:54,500 --> 00:23:59,500
It being whatever the problem is that we need to resolve,

317
00:23:59,500 --> 00:24:02,500
whatever the goal that it is that we need to reach

318
00:24:02,500 --> 00:24:04,500
or whatever challenge it is that we need to overcome.

319
00:24:04,500 --> 00:24:06,500
So there's my best advice for you.

320
00:24:06,500 --> 00:24:10,500
You're a youth leader and you want to work effectively

321
00:24:10,500 --> 00:24:13,500
with the adults who are volunteering for your troop,

322
00:24:13,500 --> 00:24:17,500
and especially if you want to make things change

323
00:24:17,500 --> 00:24:18,500
within your troop.

324
00:24:18,500 --> 00:24:20,500
It's all about respect.

325
00:24:20,500 --> 00:24:23,500
It's understanding where the adults are coming from.

326
00:24:23,500 --> 00:24:27,500
It's about the courage of learning to say things plainly

327
00:24:27,500 --> 00:24:31,500
and always attaching those things to some form of resolution,

328
00:24:31,500 --> 00:24:35,500
Some suggestion of how things can be better.

329
00:24:35,500 --> 00:24:37,500
Most importantly, it's about listening

330
00:24:37,500 --> 00:24:39,500
on both sides of the equation

331
00:24:39,500 --> 00:24:41,500
and keeping a cool head and trying to understand

332
00:24:41,500 --> 00:24:43,500
where the other guy is coming from.

333
00:24:43,500 --> 00:24:46,500
All of this not only works in the very specific situation

334
00:24:46,500 --> 00:24:48,500
that we're talking about.

335
00:24:48,500 --> 00:24:50,500
it will equip you with skills that you use

336
00:24:50,500 --> 00:24:51,500
for the rest of your life.

337
00:24:51,500 --> 00:24:53,500
I hope that helps.

338
00:24:53,500 --> 00:24:55,500
Hey, I'd love to have you get in touch with me

